---
import MarketingLayout from '../layouts/MarketingLayout.astro';
import PlaceholderMedia from '../components/PlaceholderMedia.astro';
import { withBase } from '../lib/paths';
---

<MarketingLayout title="Features" description="A beginner-friendly tour of Cove's local media organization, search, metadata, permissions, and extension system.">
  <section class="marketing-section">
    <div class="marketing-section-inner">
      <p class="eyebrow-small">Feature overview</p>
      <h1 class="page-heading">Powerful without making setup a project</h1>
      <p class="page-copy">Cove is built to feel approachable on day one and still be the place you want your library years later. Here's what that looks like in practice.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="marketing-section">
    <div class="marketing-section-inner setup-grid">
      <article class="setup-card step-card">
        <h3>Install</h3>
        <p>Use Docker or a native build. Cove bundles or auto-manages the pieces most people do not want to assemble by hand.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="setup-card step-card">
        <h3>Scan</h3>
        <p>Add a folder, generate previews, index files, and let Cove create the first useful view of your collection.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="setup-card step-card">
        <h3>Organize</h3>
        <p>Add tags, performers, studios, groups, saved filters, covers, and metadata as you go. A small library can stay simple.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="setup-card step-card">
        <h3>Grow</h3>
        <p>Turn on users, permissions, downloaders, AI workflows, extensions, and deeper automation when you actually need them.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="marketing-section">
    <div class="marketing-section-inner two-column">
      <div class="panel">
        <p class="section-kicker">Library foundation</p>
        <h3>More than a video list</h3>
        <p>Cove treats scenes, images, galleries, audio, text, faces, segments, groups, performers, studios, tags, and generated media as parts of the same library. That matters when your collection stops fitting into one narrow category.</p>
        <ul class="feature-list">
          <li>Scenes, images, galleries, audio, and text are first-class media.</li>
          <li>Tags, tag groups, custom fields, performers, studios, and groups keep structure reusable.</li>
          <li>Segments and virtual compilations let you organize moments inside media, including who appears when, without duplicating files.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <PlaceholderMedia label="Unified library overview" caption="Scenes, images, galleries, audio, text, groups, and saved views." />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="marketing-section">
    <div class="marketing-section-inner feature-grid">
      <article class="feature-card"><h3>Search that goes past titles</h3><p>Search across titles, descriptions, paths, tags, aliases, performers, studios, groups, and saved criteria. Boolean search, quoted phrases, exclusions, regex modifiers, and saved filters are there when you need more precision.</p><a href={withBase('docs/user/library/search-and-filters/')}>Search docs</a></article>
      <article class="feature-card"><h3>Metadata with a memory</h3><p>Scrapers, metadata servers, imports, AI, and manual edits can all touch your library. Cove keeps a record of which one set each field, so you can compare sources and understand why your library looks the way it does.</p><a href={withBase('docs/user/metadata/provenance/')}>Metadata history docs</a></article>
      <article class="feature-card"><h3>Contextual tags</h3><p>Tags do not have to stop at the whole scene. Cove can attach them to a specific performer, face, detection, or segment when that extra context matters.</p><a href={withBase('docs/user/library/organizing/')}>Organizing docs</a></article>
      <article class="feature-card"><h3>Real users, not a shared login</h3><p>Run Cove for yourself, a household, or a small team. Users, groups, roles, content rules, share links, API tokens, and audit history let people share one library without sharing one account.</p><a href={withBase('docs/user/security/users-roles-permissions/')}>Security docs</a></article>
      <article class="feature-card"><h3>Downloader platform</h3><p>Cove includes a first-class downloader platform, so compatible downloaders can match URLs and download files directly into your library.</p><a href={withBase('docs/user/library/organizing/')}>Organizing docs</a></article>
      <article class="feature-card"><h3>Extensions that feel native</h3><p>Add backend jobs, UI pages, tabs, slots, dialogs, settings, persistent data, permissions, APIs, and themes through Cove's extension system.</p><a href={withBase('docs/developer/extensions/overview/')}>Developer docs</a></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="marketing-section">
    <div class="marketing-section-inner">
      <p class="section-kicker">If you've used a local organizer before</p>
      <h2 class="section-heading">It's not one feature. It's the ceiling.</h2>
      <p class="section-copy">If you already know what local media organization can do, Cove is built around the next set of expectations: shared libraries that respect users, metadata you can audit, segments and contextual tags as part of the same model, and extensions that reach the entire app.</p>
      <div class="comparison-grid">
        <article class="comparison-card"><span class="before">A library for more than one person</span><span class="after">Users, groups, and roles — plus content rules, share links, API tokens, and audit history — instead of a single shared login.</span></article>
        <article class="comparison-card"><span class="before">Metadata you can audit</span><span class="after">Each field tracks which scraper, import, edit, or extension set it, so the result is explainable.</span></article>
        <article class="comparison-card"><span class="before">Segments as a real concept</span><span class="after">Sub-scenes, time-range tags, performer context, generated previews, and virtual compilations all live in one model.</span></article>
        <article class="comparison-card"><span class="before">Extensions across the whole app</span><span class="after">UI pages, APIs, jobs, events, settings, persistent data, permissions, and themes are all part of the same extension system.</span></article>
        <article class="comparison-card"><span class="before">More media types in one place</span><span class="after">Scenes, images, galleries, audio, and text are first-class — with shared search, tagging, and grouping.</span></article>
        <article class="comparison-card"><span class="before">Built to keep up</span><span class="after">PostgreSQL-backed search and explicit performance targets in the server and the UI.</span></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="marketing-section">
    <div class="marketing-section-inner two-column">
      <div class="panel">
        <p class="section-kicker">Personal workspace</p>
        <h3>Make Cove feel like your library</h3>
        <p>The UI is designed to be quick to scan, comfortable for repeated use, and customizable enough to match how you browse. Start with one of 19 built-in themes, then tune the details.</p>
        <div class="theme-strip" aria-label="Theme color samples">
          <span class="theme-swatch" style="--swatch-a:#071019;--swatch-b:#ff2f9a"></span>
          <span class="theme-swatch" style="--swatch-a:#10251f;--swatch-b:#33d17a"></span>
          <span class="theme-swatch" style="--swatch-a:#1a1526;--swatch-b:#9d7bff"></span>
          <span class="theme-swatch" style="--swatch-a:#f7f2e9;--swatch-b:#276ef1"></span>
          <span class="theme-swatch" style="--swatch-a:#201414;--swatch-b:#ff7043"></span>
          <span class="theme-swatch" style="--swatch-a:#101820;--swatch-b:#13b8c2"></span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <PlaceholderMedia label="Theme gallery" caption="Theme picker, custom colors, layout styles, and interface preferences." />
    </div>
  </section>
</MarketingLayout>
